{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-fermion-boson-knot-link","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:fermion-boson-knot-link","kind":"proposition","title":"Fermions are knots, bosons are links","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-borromean-baryon"],"tags":["fermion","boson","knot","link","torus-knot","component","reeb-flow","vertical"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.Braiding.fermion_boson_knot_link","text":"fermion-boson-knot-link.md","leanSource":"fermion-boson-knot-link.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"prop:fermion-boson-knot-link\"></a>\n<strong>Proposition.</strong> <em>Fermions are knots, bosons are links</em></p>\n<p>The fermion--boson decomposition coincides with the knot--link decomposition of the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T(2,n)</code> torus family on Bring's surface:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>fermion</strong> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\alpha_V \\neq 0</code>) is a <strong>knot</strong> (1 component).</li>\n<li>A <strong>boson</strong> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\alpha_V = 0</code>) is a <strong>link</strong> (2 components).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Equivalently, fermions have odd <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n</code> in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T(2,n)</code> and bosons have even <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n</code>.</p>\n"}
